{"name":"Timber Trail","hex":"#a1755c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"timber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Trees in a forest regarded as a source of wood.","Wood that has been pre-cut and is ready for use in construction."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used by loggers to warn others that a tree being felled is falling.","A cry used when anything is falling over."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/timber"},{"term":"trail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To follow behind (someone or something); to tail (someone or something).","To drag (something) behind on the ground."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The track or indication marking the route followed by something that has passed, such as the footprints of animal on land or the contrail of an airplane in the sky.","A route for travel over land, especially a narrow, unpaved pathway for use by hikers, horseback riders, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Timber Trail","blocks":[{"text":"The Timber Trail, originally known as the Central North Island Rail Trail or Pureora Timber Trail, in the North Island of New Zealand is an 84-kilometre (52 mi) cycleway in Pureora Forest Park, fully opened in 2013, with 35 bridges, including eight large suspension bridges. It is one of several cycleways developed as part of the New Zealand Cycle Trail and passes through some of the last remaining podocarp forests of rimu, tōtara, miro, mataī and kahikatea, as well as some exotic forestry and regenerating bush. About half the trail is on the track-bed of the old Ellis and Burnand Tramway, including a spiral and tunnel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Timber_Trail.jpg/330px-Timber_Trail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timber_Trail"},{"title":"Lumber","blocks":[{"text":"Lumber, also called timber or fine wood in the United Kingdom, Australia, and New Zealand, is wood that has been processed into uniform and useful sizes, including beams and planks or boards. Lumber is mainly used for construction framing, as well as finishing. Lumber has many uses beyond home building. In some parts of the world, including the United States and Canada, the term timber refers specifically to unprocessed wood fiber, such as cut logs or standing trees that have yet to be cut."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Wood_from_victoria_mountain_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_from_victoria_mountain_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lumber"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/115m_Bog_Creek_suspension_bridge.JPG/500px-115m_Bog_Creek_suspension_bridge.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/115m_Bog_Creek_suspension_bridge.JPG/1280px-115m_Bog_Creek_suspension_bridge.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/115m_Bog_Creek_suspension_bridge.JPG/500px-115m_Bog_Creek_suspension_bridge.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"115-metre (377 ft) Bog Creek bridge","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A115m_Bog_Creek_suspension_bridge.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Maramataha_Bridge_opened_2011.JPG/500px-Maramataha_Bridge_opened_2011.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Maramataha_Bridge_opened_2011.JPG/1280px-Maramataha_Bridge_opened_2011.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Maramataha_Bridge_opened_2011.JPG/500px-Maramataha_Bridge_opened_2011.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"141-metre (463 ft) Maramataha Bridge over the Maramataha River","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaramataha_Bridge_opened_2011.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Cutting_on_the_Ellis_and_Burnand_Ongarue_tramway%2C_now_the_Timber_Trail.JPG/500px-Cutting_on_the_Ellis_and_Burnand_Ongarue_tramway%2C_now_the_Timber_Trail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Cutting_on_the_Ellis_and_Burnand_Ongarue_tramway%2C_now_the_Timber_Trail.JPG/1280px-Cutting_on_the_Ellis_and_Burnand_Ongarue_tramway%2C_now_the_Timber_Trail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Cutting_on_the_Ellis_and_Burnand_Ongarue_tramway%2C_n